{#- modules/governance/what-is-governance.j2
    Governance, defined in the plainest terms the room already agrees with.

    A security audience recognises the word "governance" and has usually been
    handed a framework-shaped definition of it. This says the same thing in
    three steps they can hold for the rest of the talk, and those three steps
    are the deck's spine: write the rule, put it where the work happens, then
    check. Everything after this hangs off one of the three.

    Deliberately no PAP/PDP/PEP, no XACML, no control-family vocabulary. The
    architecture underneath is real and old, but naming it here costs the room
    the thread and buys nothing they will use on Monday.

    ── One word, one definition, three boxes ──────────────────────
    The subtitle is the definition Jon has used since the 2023 and 2024 "Your
    Governance Is Broken" talks (modules/governance/governance_intro*.html),
    where the surrounding words faded and "making and enforcing decisions" was
    what stayed on screen. Keep the wording verbatim; the room that saw those
    talks should recognise it. Set plain and large — an earlier version bolded
    the middle clause, which read as a pull quote competing with the boxes.

    Note the definition covers "make" and "enforce" but not "update". The third
    box is the one this talk argues everybody is guessing at, so the boxes
    deliberately say more than the sentence above them does. That gap is a
    thing to say out loud, not to paper over by editing the definition.

    Nothing else on the slide: no callout, no kicker. The three labels are the
    argument, and every further line of chrome made the room read instead of
    listen.
    An earlier version carried a heading plus a second slide layering the
    AI-native tooling on top; both were cut. Keep it bare — the speaker says
    the rest.

    Written against the brand-pack primitive contract (see AGENTS.md): no hex
    colors, no brand class names. Import WITH CONTEXT:

      {%- from "modules/governance/what-is-governance.j2" import
          what_it_is with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide,
    slide_heading,
    pipe
-%}


{#- The three steps. Labels only — no detail lines. The whole point of the
    slide is that each phrase is short enough to land as it appears, and a
    sentence under each one turned it into something to read rather than hear.
    The speaker says the rest.

    No `note` or `below` key: those reserve an annotation gutter in every
    pack's `pipe()`, which would push the row off the vertical centre. -#}
{%- set STEPS = [
  {"label": "Make the rules",    "accent": "primary"},
  {"label": "Enforce the rules", "accent": "info"},
  {"label": "Update the rules",  "accent": "success"},
] -%}


{%- macro what_it_is() -%}
{#- reveal="stages": the three boxes arrive ONE AT A TIME, each with the arrow
    that leads to it, so the definition is walked through a beat at a time
    rather than dumped. `.step` uses visibility:hidden, so all three positions
    are held from the start and nothing shifts as they appear. Three presses,
    then the slide advances.

    size="lg": with no detail text under the labels, the boxes carry the slide
    and need the type. -#}
{%- call content_slide("What governance is") %}
  {#- Sized in `em` so it scales with whatever each pack sets for a subtitle,
      rather than pinning a px value that only suits one theme. No bold: the
      sentence is read aloud, and emphasis markup made it look like a pull
      quote competing with the three boxes. -#}
  {{ slide_heading("", "Governance",
                   '<span style="font-size:1.5em; line-height:1.35;">Governance is the process of
                    making and enforcing decisions within an organization.</span>') }}
  {{ pipe(STEPS, reveal="stages", size="lg") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}
